WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:21.296
The woman invited me into her house and said, "Take a chair."

2
00:00:22.136 --> 00:00:22.796
I sat down.

3
00:00:23.276 --> 00:00:25.497
She looked at me closely and asked my name.

4
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:28.859
"Sarah Williams," I answered.

5
00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:35.695
Then she asked where I was from, and I told her I came from Hookerville, about ten kilometers

6
00:00:35.755 --> 00:00:41.600
away. I said that my mother was sick and I had come to look for my uncle, Abner

7
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.425
Moore. She explained that she had been living in the town for only two weeks and didn't know

8
00:00:47.506 --> 00:00:47.926
my uncle.

9
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:53.491
She invited me to stay the night, but I said that I would just rest for a while

10
00:00:53.851 --> 00:00:54.491
and then leave.

11
00:00:55.913 --> 00:00:58.935
Then she began to talk about the recent murder in St.

12
00:00:58.995 --> 00:01:02.457
Petersburg. "Who killed Huckleberry Finn?"

13
00:01:03.137 --> 00:01:03.518
I asked.

14
00:01:03.538 --> 00:01:10.721
"Some people think it was his father," she said, "and some people think it was Jim, a runaway

15
00:01:10.821 --> 00:01:15.764
slave. There's a reward for catching the slave—three hundred dollars.

16
00:01:17.105 --> 00:01:20.307
After the boy was murdered, old Finn came into town.

17
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:25.309
He cried and begged Judge Thatcher for some money so he could hunt for the slave.

18
00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:30.272
The last time anyone saw him, he was with some very tough-looking men.

19
00:01:31.068 --> 00:01:36.433
Some people think old Finn killed his son so that he could get his money, and that he'll

20
00:01:36.473 --> 00:01:37.434
come back for it."

21
00:01:37.454 --> 00:01:41.697
"Does that mean they've stopped hunting for the slave?"

22
00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:42.558
I asked.

23
00:01:42.578 --> 00:01:44.880
"Oh no," she said.

24
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.562
"Some people still think Jim did it.

25
00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:50.605
And they would like that three hundred dollar reward.

26
00:01:51.446 --> 00:01:54.508
My husband and his friends are out looking for Jim right now.

27
00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:58.292
I told my husband he should look on Jackson's Island.

28
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.243
I've seen smoke over there at night, and I think the slave is hiding there."

29
00:02:05.443 --> 00:02:07.625
I felt very nervous when she said this.

30
00:02:08.665 --> 00:02:11.867
I needed something to do so that she would not see how scared I was.

31
00:02:13.047 --> 00:02:16.009
I saw some sewing supplies sitting on the table beside me.

32
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:20.952
I picked up a needle from the table and tried to thread it, but I didn't do a

33
00:02:20.992 --> 00:02:21.472
good job.

34
00:02:22.953 --> 00:02:26.755
The woman watched me, and finally she said, "What's your real name?

35
00:02:27.095 --> 00:02:27.675
Is it Bill?

36
00:02:28.136 --> 00:02:31.869
Tom?" I started to shake and didn't know what to say.

37
00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:38.231
But she said kindly, "I think you are a poor runaway boy who has been treated badly.

38
00:02:39.431 --> 00:02:44.712
I'll keep your secret, and I'll give you some food for your journey, but I don't think you

39
00:02:44.753 --> 00:02:46.453
should try to pretend to be a girl.

40
00:02:47.253 --> 00:02:51.835
When you thread a needle, you are supposed to hold the needle still and bring the thread to

41
00:02:51.915 --> 00:02:56.596
it. You brought the needle to the thread, and no girl would do that."

42
00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:02.907
I thanked the woman and left her house as quickly as I could, running back to the canoe.

43
00:03:03.768 --> 00:03:06.750
I paddled over to the island and climbed up to our cave.

44
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.991
"Jim, wake up!"

45
00:03:09.532 --> 00:03:09.912
I cried.

46
00:03:09.932 --> 00:03:12.094
"We have to leave right away.

47
00:03:12.834 --> 00:03:13.595
They're after us!"

48
00:03:16.257 --> 00:03:19.259
It was about one in the morning by the time we left the island.

49
00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:23.061
We were on the big raft we had found a few days before.

50
00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:26.904
We had piled all our supplies and our canoe on it.

51
00:03:27.828 --> 00:03:32.152
When daylight came, we tied the raft up on the riverbank and hid it with branches.

52
00:03:33.693 --> 00:03:38.538
Then we lay in the shade all day and watched the big steamboats going up and down the

53
00:03:38.578 --> 00:03:42.281
river. I wondered if the men had found our campsite yet.

54
00:03:43.242 --> 00:03:46.425
Just before we left, I had built a campfire to fool them.

55
00:03:47.546 --> 00:03:51.189
I hoped they had sat by it all night, waiting for Jim to come back.

56
00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.532
We spent every night on our raft.

57
00:03:55.372 --> 00:03:58.934
Jim used some planks to make a little hut for us in the middle of the raft.

58
00:03:59.774 --> 00:04:04.256
The hut had a raised floor, and in the middle, we made a pit and filled it with

59
00:04:04.396 --> 00:04:07.677
earth. Here we could build a fire if we got cold.

60
00:04:09.878 --> 00:04:12.979
Every night we traveled, floating downriver with the current.

61
00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.801
On the fifth night, we passed the great city of St.

62
00:04:16.841 --> 00:04:19.642
Louis, which looked like the whole world lit up.

63
00:04:20.783 --> 00:04:24.004
It was completely silent though—all the people were asleep.

64
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.681
We had lots of food to eat.

65
00:04:27.722 --> 00:04:33.945
In the early morning, before the sun came up, I would sneak ashore into a farmer's field and

66
00:04:33.985 --> 00:04:35.826
take a watermelon or some corn.

67
00:04:37.007 --> 00:04:40.910
Every evening I would go into a town to buy a little cornmeal or bacon.

68
00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.411
Sometimes I shot a duck.

69
00:04:44.972 --> 00:04:47.013
One night there was a big thunderstorm.

70
00:04:48.034 --> 00:04:51.376
We stayed inside the hut as the rain poured down on our raft.

71
00:04:52.384 --> 00:04:56.646
Flashes of lightning lit up the river, and suddenly I saw something ahead.

72
00:04:57.706 --> 00:05:00.067
It was a wrecked steamboat caught on a rock.

73
00:05:00.968 --> 00:05:04.729
I wanted to go aboard and have a look around, but Jim was against it.

74
00:05:04.749 --> 00:05:08.471
"Maybe there's someone on board," he said.

75
00:05:08.491 --> 00:05:12.552
"No one will be on a wreck like that," I argued.

76
00:05:12.572 --> 00:05:15.213
"And we might find some supplies on board."

77
00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:21.056
We tied our raft up to the boat, and I explored it while Jim waited by the raft.

78
00:05:22.364 --> 00:05:26.549
Suddenly I heard a frightened voice crying, "Oh, please don't, boys.

79
00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.171
I swear I will never tell."
